Gutter-Gourmet

Creative Catalyst: The movement ignited when underground hackers hijacked city traffic grids to loop snare-heavy protest rhythms through every red light’s sonic signal.

The sound is a frantic, jagged collision between the oppressive, repetitive grind of industrial techno and the erratic, high-energy syncopation of barking-duck electro-swing. It replaces the "party" aesthetic of swing with a cold, dystopian hunger, utilizing chopped-up scat-vocals that sound like glitching automated security announcements. It is abrasive, danceable, and deeply uncomfortable, favoring a "broken" melodic sensibility where jazz-age brass instruments are forced through industrial crushing filters until they sound like rusty machinery begging for grease.

Genre DNA
*   Tempo & Pulse: 155–170 BPM; characterized by a "stutter-kick" where the 4/4 industrial floor-tom is interrupted by rapid-fire, uneven jazz-drum fills.
*   Harmonic Palette: Diminished-seventh chords transposed into dissonant, non-tempered scales, favoring the sound of heavy metal shearing under pressure.
*   Production Signatures: 
    1. Traffic-Gate Compression: Using live, recorded telemetry from urban grids as a side-chain trigger to create rhythmic "dips" in audio fidelity. 
    2. Brass-Corrosion Modeling: Passing sample-heavy clarinet and trumpet lines through a physical-modeling plugin that simulates metal oxidation. 
    3. Glitch-Scatting: Chopping vocal scat sequences so aggressively they lose their pitch and become pure percussive "clucks" and "barks."
*   Dynamics: A non-stop, hyper-dense build; songs never reach a traditional climax, instead decaying into the sound of digital clipping and mechanical fatigue.

Scene & Origin Lore
Born in the ventilation tunnels of a sprawling megalopolis, Gutter-Gourmet is the music of the "Traffic-Tappers"—outcasts who view the city’s grid as a giant, unresponsive musical instrument. They wear repurposed reflective road-worker gear, now encrusted with discarded cutlery and copper wiring. Their rituals involve "feasting" on raw, stolen data—hooking laptops to traffic cameras to watch the city flow while they broadcast their sets over the grid. It is an aesthetic of tactical clutter, where a vintage brass trombone is held together by duct tape and high-voltage caution tape, reflecting the brutal reality of their hand-to-mouth existence in the shadow of the smart-city.

Central Instruments: Distorted trombone, granular-synthesized duck-call samples, modded modular synths (patch-cabled to traffic signal controllers), heavily processed jazz-piano samples, industrial percussion.

For Fans Of: Venetian Snares, Igorrr, Death Grips, Caravan Palace (on a bad trip).

